[Event "Ruy Lopez: the main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/BbL9IxEf"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Welcome to the Ruy Lopez, the Spanish game: your bishop leans on the knight that guards his e pawn, and the pressure never lets up. Start with the king pawn. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Your pawn grabs a share of the middle and opens roads for your queen and your light bishop. [%cal Gf1b5,Gd1h5] } (1. d4 { The queen pawn is just as good a start, but it leads to a very different game. } 1... d5 2. c4 { That is the queen gambit, calm and strong. The Ruy Lopez needs the king pawn first. [%csl Bc4] }) (1. Nf3 { A fine, flexible start that keeps your plans hidden a little longer. } 1... d5 2. d4 { A quiet queen pawn game. Good chess, but today the king pawn leads. [%csl Bd4] }) (1. c4 { The English opening: sound, and played from the side of the board. } 1... e5 2. Nc3 { A slower, roomier kind of game. Today we want the king pawn in the middle. [%csl Bc4] }) 1... e5 { He answers with his own king pawn. Against c5 or e6 you would be in a different opening. Bring your king knight out and attack his pawn. [%cal Yg1f3] } 2. Nf3 { The knight hits his pawn on e5 and clears the way for castling. [%cal Gf3e5] } (2. Nc3 { A good move, but this knight attacks nothing yet. } 2... Nf6 { He is happy to develop too. A calm game, and not the Ruy Lopez, which starts with the king knight. [%csl Bc3] }) (2. Bc4 { The bishop first is fine, but it gives him a free move to hit e4. } 2... Nf6 3. d3 { Your pawn has to guard e4, and the game is quiet. The knight to f3 attacks first. [%cal Bd3e4] }) (2. d4 { Opening the middle this early drags your queen out. } 2... exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 { His knight hits your queen on d4, and she has to move a second time. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (2. f4 { The king gambit: a pawn thrown at e5 before your pieces are out. } 2... exf4 3. Nf3 { He took the pawn, and your king side is wide open. That is another study. [%csl Rf4] }) 2... Nc6 { His knight guards e5. A knight move to f6 instead would have been another opening. Now the move that names this one: your bishop goes straight at that knight. [%cal Yf1b5,Bc6e5] } 3. Bb5 { That is the Ruy Lopez: your bishop attacks the knight on c6, and that knight is the only guard of e5. [%cal Gb5c6,Gc6e5] } (3. Bc4 { The bishop to c4 is the Italian game, a fine opening that aims at f7 instead. } 3... Bc5 4. c3 { A different fight, with a study of its own. Here your bishop belongs on b5. [%csl Bc4] }) (3. d4 { Breaking open the middle at once is the Scotch game, just as good. } 3... exd4 4. Nxd4 { Your knight takes back in the middle: a lively game, but not the Ruy Lopez. [%csl Bd4] }) (3. Nc3 { The second knight comes out. Fine, and very calm. } 3... Nf6 4. Bb5 { Your bishop reaches b5 a move later, and his knight is already on f6. Level. [%csl Bf6] }) 3... a6 { He asks your bishop to decide at once. Keep the pressure: step back to a4, still looking at the knight. [%cal Yb5a4,Ga4c6] } 4. Ba4 { From a4 the bishop still stares at the knight on c6. It can take there any time, so there is no hurry. [%cal Ga4c6] } (4. Bxc6 { Taking at once is a real opening too, but it gives up the bishop straight away. } 4... dxc6 5. O-O { Level: his pawns on c7 and c6 are doubled, but he has two bishops for the long game. [%csl Rc6,Rc7] }) (4. Bc4 { Back to c4? The bishop has used two moves, and he got a6 for free. } 4... Nf6 5. d3 { Level, but you are playing the Italian a move behind. [%csl Bc4] }) 4... Nf6 { His knight hits e4. Castle anyway: if he grabs the pawn, your d pawn wins it back. Had he played d6 instead, you would castle just the same. [%cal Ye1g1,Rf6e4] } 5. O-O { Your king is safe on g1, and your rook on f1 is ready to join the middle. [%csl Gg1] } (5. d3 { Guarding e4 with a pawn is solid and playable, just a touch slower. [%cal Bd3e4] } 5... Bc5 6. O-O { Level, and quieter: his bishop found a good home on c5. [%csl Bc5] }) (5. Nc3 { The knight guards e4, but it stands on the square your c pawn wants. [%cal Bc3e4] } 5... Be7 6. O-O { Level, but c3 and d4 are harder to play now. [%csl Rc3] }) (5. Qe2 { The queen guards e4. Playable, but she stands in front of your king for a while. [%cal Be2e4] } 5... b5 6. Bb3 { Level. Castling first keeps the queen free to choose her square later. [%csl Be2] }) (5. d4 { Striking in the middle before castling opens lines while your king is still at home. } 5... exd4 6. O-O Be7 7. e5 Ne4 8. Nxd4 { Level: the pawn is back, but his knight sits well on e4. Castling first is simpler. [%csl Re4] }) (5. Bxc6 { Take the knight and then e5? Not yet: nothing guards your pawn on e4. } 5... dxc6 6. Nxe5 Qd4 7. Nf3 Qxe4+ 8. Qe2 Qxe2+ 9. Kxe2 { The pawn came straight back with check, and your king can never castle now. [%csl Re2] }) 5... Be7 { He tucks his bishop away, ready to castle. Now your rook to e1: once your king pawn is guarded, taking on c6 really does win his e pawn. [%cal Yf1e1] } 6. Re1 { Now e4 is guarded, and the threat is real: your bishop takes his knight, then your knight takes e5. [%cal Ga4c6,Gf3e5,Ge1e4] } (6. d3 { Guarding e4 with the pawn is just as good, and a little quieter. [%cal Bd3e4] } 6... b5 7. Bb3 d6 { Level and solid, though the pawn on d3 needs a second step to reach d4. [%csl Bd3] }) (6. Nc3 { The knight guards e4 too, but c3 is where your pawn wants to go. [%cal Bc3e4] } 6... b5 7. Bb3 { Level, but the c3 and d4 plan is blocked by your own knight. [%csl Rc3] }) (6. Bxc6 { Taking and grabbing e5 here? Not yet: nothing guards e4. [%csl Re4] } 6... dxc6 7. Nxe5 Nxe4 { The pawn comes straight back: with no rook on e1, your pawn on e4 was loose. [%csl Re4] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen guards e4 instead, which is playable. [%cal Be2e4] } 6... b5 7. Bb3 { Level. The rook does the same job and keeps your queen at home. [%csl Be2] }) 6... b5 { He sees the threat and kicks your bishop. Take it back to b3. [%cal Ya4b3] } 7. Bb3 { From b3 the bishop looks down the diagonal to f7, right beside his king. [%cal Gb3f7] } (7. Nxe5 { Grab e5 now? Your bishop is still attacked. } 7... Nxe5 8. d4 Ng6 9. Bb3 { You won a pawn and lost a knight: his knight escaped to g6. [%csl Rg6] }) 7... d6 { He guards e5 with a pawn. Now a quiet move that matters a lot: the c pawn to c3. [%cal Yc2c3,Bd6e5] } 8. c3 { The c pawn does two jobs: it backs up a push to d4, and it leaves c2 free as a safe home for your bishop. [%cal Gc3d4,Gb3c2] } (8. a4 { Hitting his b5 pawn is just as good. } 8... b4 9. c3 { Level: your a pawn made him commit. We drill c3 first to keep the plan simple. [%csl Ba4] }) (8. h3 { A useful move, but too early: your c pawn still blocks c2. } 8... Na5 9. d3 Nxb3 10. axb3 { His knight swapped itself for your bishop, which had no square to run to. [%csl Rb3] }) (8. d4 { Striking at once looks natural, but it lets him trade on d4. } 8... Nxd4 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Qxd4 c5 11. Qd3 c4 12. Bxc4 bxc4 13. Qxc4 { Taking back with the queen was the real mistake: his c pawn trapped your bishop, and you got two pawns for it. [%csl Rb3] }) (8. a3 { Making room on a2 for the bishop is playable. } 8... O-O 9. c3 { Level: a3 made a home for your bishop, but c3 does that job too and backs up the d pawn push as well. [%csl Ba3] }) 8... O-O { He castles. One more little pawn move: h3, so his bishop can never pin your knight from g4. [%cal Yh2h3,Rc8g4] } 9. h3 { Your set-up is complete: king safe, rook behind the e pawn, bishop eyeing f7, and g4 shut to his bishop. That is the Ruy Lopez. [%csl Gg4][%cal Gb3f7,Ge1e4] } (9. d4 { Just as good: the centre strike at once, but it lets his bishop come to g4. } 9... Bg4 10. d5 { Level. His bishop on g4 pins your knight to your queen, from the very square h3 would have taken away. [%cal Rg4d1] }) (9. a4 { The a pawn push at b5 is just as good. } 9... b4 10. a5 { Level: your a pawn fixes his queen side pawns, a plan for another day. [%csl Ba5] }) (9. d3 { Guarding e4 again is quiet, and his knight still chases your bishop. [%cal Bd3e4] } 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 { Level and solid, but the pawn to d4 in one step gives you more room. [%csl Bd3] }) (9. Bc2 { Tucking the bishop away before he asks lets him strike first. } 9... d5 10. d4 Nxe4 { Level, but he opened the middle on his terms and took your e pawn to start with. [%csl Re4] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: he hits e4 at once"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/16tC5qP2"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]

{ Your bishop on b5 leans on his knight, the knight that guards e5. [%cal Bc6e5] }
3... Nf6 { He skips a6 and hits your king pawn at once. Had he played d6, your d pawn would strike. Here: does that pawn really need a guard? } 4. O-O { Right: castle and let him look at e4. Your king is safe, and the pawn is not lost for long. [%csl Gg1] } (4. d3 { Guarding e4 with a pawn is a fine, quiet choice. [%cal Bd3e4] } 4... Bc5 5. c3 { Level and calm: his bishop sits well on c5. Castling first keeps more fight in the position. [%csl Bc5] }) (4. Nc3 { The knight guards e4, but his knight jumps to d4. [%cal Bc3e4] } 4... Nd4 5. Nxd4 exd4 { Level, but his pawn on d4 hits your knight on c3, which has to move again. [%cal Rd4c3] }) (4. Qe2 { The queen guards e4. Playable, but she is out early. [%cal Be2e4] } 4... Bc5 5. O-O { Level: he developed freely while your queen did a pawn's job. [%csl Be2] }) (4. d4 { Striking at once lets him take on d4, and his knight finds a fine square. } 4... exd4 5. e5 Ne4 { Level: you are a pawn down for now, and his knight sits proudly on e4. [%csl Re4] }) 4... Nxe4 { He takes the pawn. Against a bishop move you would build with your c and d pawns; against d6, strike with your d pawn at once. Now, how do you get this pawn back? } 5. Re1 { The rook swings to e1 and attacks his knight on e4, with your king tucked away behind it. [%cal Ge1e4] } (5. d4 { Just as good, and the most played: it leads to a game without queens. } 5... Nd6 6. Bxc6 dxc6 7. dxe5 Nf5 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 { The queens are gone and his king stands on d8, never to castle. Level, and a long game. We drill the rook. [%csl Rd8] }) (5. Nxe5 { Grabbing a pawn back at once walks into a knight trade that costs you a piece for a while. } 5... Nxe5 6. Re1 f5 7. d3 a6 8. dxe4 axb5 9. exf5 d6 10. f4 Bxf5 11. fxe5 { After all the swaps material is level, but his bishops rake an open board and your pawns are loose. He is better. [%csl Rf5] }) (5. Qe2 { The queen hits the knight too, but it hops away with a threat. } 5... Ng5 6. Nxg5 Qxg5 { Level, but his queen came out with a gain of time, and your pawn is not back yet. [%csl Rg5] }) 5... Nd6 { His knight steps back and attacks your bishop on b5. Before you move the bishop, look around the board. } 6. Nxe5 { Good: your knight takes e5, and the material is level again. The bishop can wait a move. [%cal Gf3e5] } (6. Bxc6 { Trading on c6 first hands him the two bishops. } 6... dxc6 7. Nxe5 Be7 { Material is level, but he has two bishops and open lines for them. Slightly easier for him. [%csl Bc8,Be7] }) (6. Bf1 { Saving the bishop first is safe, but his pawn on e5 gets a guard. } 6... f6 7. d4 Nf7 { Level: you are a pawn down, but your pieces are quicker. Taking e5 at once was simpler. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Ba4 { Keeping the bishop on the diagonal gives him time. } 6... Be7 7. Nc3 O-O { Level, but you are still a pawn short, with quicker pieces as your only payment. [%csl Re5] }) (6. a4 { Guarding the bishop with a pawn is clever but slow. } 6... Be7 7. Nc3 a6 { Level: his bishop is out, and your e pawn is still missing. [%csl Ba4] }) 6... Be7 { He brings his bishop out to castle. Had he taken on e5, rook takes with check and you reach the same place. Where does your bishop go now? } 7. Bf1 { Home to f1, safe and guarding your king. Your rook backs up the knight on e5. [%cal Ge1e5] } (7. Nxc6 { Trading knights first is fine, and a little tamer. } 7... dxc6 8. Bf1 { Level: his pawns are split, but his two bishops have open roads. [%csl Bc6] }) (7. Bd3 { The bishop on d3 blocks your own d pawn. } 7... Nxe5 8. Rxe5 O-O { Level, but your d pawn is stuck behind the bishop, and your queen side cannot get out easily. [%csl Rd2] }) (7. Ba4 { From a4 the bishop can be hit again by his b pawn. } 7... Nxe5 8. Rxe5 O-O { Level, but b5 is always there to chase the bishop, and f1 was its safe home. [%csl Ra4] }) 7... Nxe5 { He swaps knights on e5, so take it back. Had his knight hopped to f5 instead, yours would step back. } 8. Rxe5 { The pawn is back: he took e4, you took e5, and material is level. His knight sits in front of his own d pawn and locks in his light bishop. [%csl Rd6,Rd7,Rc8] } 1-0


