[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/1B2p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]

{ Your bishop on b5 leans on his knight, the only guard of his pawn on e5. }
3... f5 { He throws his f pawn forward, and it attacks your pawn on e4. Taking it is risky. What is the calm answer? } 4. d3 { Calm and strong: the pawn on d3 guards e4, so if he takes, you take back and nothing is lost. [%cal Gd3e4] } (4. Nc3 { Just as good, and the most played, but it leads to sharp, forcing fights. } 4... fxe4 5. Nxe4 { Level: your knight took back on e4, and sharp lines follow. The pawn to d3 keeps it simple. [%csl Be4] }) (4. exf5 { Taking the pawn lets his e pawn jump forward and hit your knight. } 4... e4 5. Qe2 Qe7 { You are a pawn up, but your knight was pushed around and his pawn on e4 cramps you. He is slightly better. [%csl Re4] }) (4. Bxc6 { Trading first is playable, but it hands him the two bishops. } 4... dxc6 5. Nc3 { Level: his bishops have open roads, and he never has to worry about e5. [%csl Bc8,Bf8] }) 4... fxe4 { He takes on e4, so take it back. Had he brought his knight to f6 first, castling would lead to this same position. } 5. dxe4 { Material is level again, and the pawn from f7, right beside his king, has left home. [%csl Gf7] } 5... Nf6 { His knight comes out and eyes your pawn on e4. } 6. O-O { Your king is safe. And e4 is not really hanging: if his knight takes it, your rook comes to e1 and hits it. [%cal Gf1e1] } (6. Nc3 { Guarding e4 with the knight is natural, but his bishop pins it at once. [%cal Bc3e4] } 6... Bb4 7. Qd3 Bxc3+ 8. Qxc3 Nxe4 { He swapped off your knight and took e4. Level, but you are the one chasing a pawn. [%csl Re4] }) (6. Bc4 { The bishop to c4 has a good idea, but he checks you first. } 6... Bb4+ 7. c3 Bc5 { Level: his bishop reached c5 anyway, and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) (6. Bg5 { Pinning his knight is natural, but he checks you first. } 6... Bb4+ 7. Nbd2 h6 { Level: your bishop is asked a question, and your king is still in the middle. [%csl Rg5] }) 6... Bc5 { His bishop comes out and stares at f2. Had he played d6, your bishop would come back to stop him castling. And here, what is your light bishop for? } 7. Bxc6 { That is the Ruy Lopez at work: the bishop takes the only guard of e5. [%cal Gb5c6,Gf3e5] } (7. Nc3 { Developing and guarding e4 is good too, and very popular. [%cal Bc3e4] } 7... d6 8. Bc4 { Slightly better for you, but his pawn on d6 now guards e5, and the free pawn is gone. [%cal Rd6e5] }) (7. Qd3 { The queen guards e4, a sensible idea. [%cal Bd3e4] } 7... Qe7 8. Nc3 { Slightly better for you, but his queen on e7 now guards e5. [%cal Re7e5] }) (7. Bg5 { Pinning his knight is fine, but it gives him a free move for d6. } 7... d6 8. Nc3 { Slightly better for you and nothing more: the pawn on d6 holds e5. [%csl Rd6] }) (7. Bc4 { The bishop on c4 stops him castling, a good idea. } 7... d6 8. Nc3 { Slightly better for you, but with d6 played there is no pawn to win on e5. [%csl Bc4] }) (7. Qe2 { The queen on e2 guards e4 as well. [%cal Be2e4] } 7... Nd4 8. Nxd4 Bxd4 { Slightly better for you, but your knight has been swapped off, and e5 is safe. [%csl Rd4] }) 7... bxc6 { He takes back with the b pawn, and e5 has lost its guard. Collect it. } 8. Nxe5 { A pawn up: his e5 pawn is gone, and your knight stands in the middle. [%csl Ge5] } (8. Qd3 { Guarding e4 first gives him time to guard e5. [%cal Bd3e4] } 8... Qe7 { His queen on e7 now guards e5, and the chance is gone. Slightly better for him, with his two bishops. [%cal Re7e5] }) (8. Nc3 { Developing first lets the free pawn go. } 8... Ba6 { His bishop hits your rook on f1, and e5 is still his. He is better. [%cal Ra6f1] }) 8... O-O { He castles first: knight takes e4 at once would have run into your queen check. Now your king pawn is under fire for real. } 9. Nd2 { The knight on d2 guards e4, and his bishop can never swap it off from there. [%cal Gd2e4] } (9. Nc3 { Just as good, and the most played. But his bishop can come to d4 and swap this guard off. } 9... d6 10. Nd3 Bd4 { Still a pawn up, but his bishop hits the knight that guards e4, and you must keep defending. [%cal Rd4c3,Gc3e4] }) (9. Bg5 { Pinning his knight is popular, but it costs your bishop. } 9... Qe8 10. Bxf6 Rxf6 11. Nd3 { Still a pawn up, but your dark bishop is gone, and his rook swings along the sixth row. [%csl Rf6] }) (9. Nd3 { Hitting his bishop at once lets him grab e4. } 9... Nxe4 10. Nxc5 Nxc5 { The pawn went back to him: material is level again. [%csl Re4] }) 9... d6 { His d pawn kicks your knight on e5. Where does it belong now? } 10. Nd3 { From d3 the knight hits his bishop and guards f2. You are a pawn up, and your other knight holds your king pawn. [%cal Gd3c5,Gd3f2,Gd2e4] } (10. Nef3 { Back to f3 looks safe, but it does nothing to his bishop. } 10... Qe7 11. Qe2 a5 { You are a pawn up, but his bishops and queen have taken over. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rc5] }) (10. Nxc6 { Grabbing c6 sends your knight deep into his camp. } 10... Qe8 11. b4 Bb6 { You are two pawns up, but your knight on c6 is stuck deep in his camp and his pieces are coming. He is better. [%csl Rc6] }) (10. Qe2 { Guarding the knight with the queen is playable. } 10... Qe8 11. Nd3 { Level: still a pawn up, but his pieces are lively, and the knight had to step back to d3 anyway. [%csl Gd3] }) 1-0


