{ Your bishop attacks the knight on c6, and that knight guards e5. [%cal Gb5c6,Bc6e5] }
3... Bc5 { He brings his bishop out, aiming at f2. Had his knight jumped to d4, you would swap knights and never grab his pawn: his queen would come out to hit your knight and your g pawn. And here? } 4. c3 { Good: the pawn on c3 prepares d4, a pawn that would hit his bishop and take the middle. [%cal Gc3d4] } (4. O-O { Castling first is just as good, and the most played. } 4... Nd4 5. Nxd4 Bxd4 { Just as good: his bishop sits on d4, and your c pawn can chase it. We drill c3 first. [%csl Bd4] }) (4. Nxe5 { Grabbing e5 looks free, but his queen hits back. } 4... Qe7 5. Nf3 Qxe4+ 6. Be2 { The pawn came back with check, and your king is stuck in the middle. Level. [%csl Re1] }) (4. d3 { Guarding e4 with d3 is solid, but it takes the punch out of your d pawn. [%cal Bd3e4] } 4... Nge7 5. O-O d6 { Level and slow: his bishop on c5 stays strong. [%csl Bc5] }) 4... Nf6 { His knight hits your king pawn. Against f5 you would strike in the middle at once, and against a knight move to e7 you would simply castle. } 5. O-O { Castle first, so your king is tucked away before the middle opens. [%csl Gg1] } (5. d4 { Just as good, and very popular, but it turns into a gambit. } 5... exd4 6. O-O dxc3 { You gave two pawns, and your pieces rush out for them: slightly better for you, but wild. Castling first keeps it calm. [%csl Rc3] }) (5. d3 { Solid, but d3 and then d4 costs an extra move. } 5... O-O 6. O-O d6 { Level and quiet: his bishop on c5 is very happy there. [%csl Bc5] }) 5... O-O { He castles as well. Grabbing e4 instead would have run into your queen coming to e2. Everything you set up is in place. } 6. d4 { There it is: your d pawn hits his bishop and his pawn on e5 at once. [%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5] } (6. Re1 { The rook to e1 is fine, and d4 can come next move. } 6... d6 7. d4 Bb6 { About level: the same centre one move later, and he had time for d6. [%csl Bd6] }) (6. d3 { Solid, but the pawn on d3 no longer hits his bishop. } 6... d6 7. Nbd2 { Level: a slow game where his bishop on c5 stays strong. [%csl Bc5] }) 6... Bb6 { His bishop steps back and keeps its eye on d4. Had he taken on d4, you would take back with the c pawn and hit the bishop again. } 7. Bg5 { The bishop pins his knight on f6 to his queen, and your pawn on d4 is well guarded. [%cal Gg5d8] } (7. a4 { Just as good: your a pawn takes room on the queen side. } 7... a6 { Level. The a pawn can go on to a5 and hit his bishop. We drill the pin. [%csl Ba4] }) (7. Re1 { A good developing move, and just as good. } 7... d6 8. h3 { About level: a slower game with the same big centre. [%csl Be1] }) (7. dxe5 { Trading pawns lets his knight jump to e4. } 7... Nxe4 8. Qd5 Nc5 { Level: his knight found good squares, and your big centre has gone. [%csl Rc5] }) (7. Bxc6 { Trading on c6 and then grabbing his pawn is possible, but he takes yours in return. } 7... dxc6 8. Nxe5 Nxe4 { Level: a pawn each, and his two bishops have open roads. [%csl Bc8] }) 7... h6 { His pawn on h6 kicks your bishop. Had he played d6 instead, the pin would simply stay. What does the bishop do now? } 8. Bh4 { Keep the pin: from h4 the bishop still ties his knight to his queen. You have two pawns in the middle against his one. [%cal Gh4d8][%csl Gd4,Ge4,Re5] } (8. Bxf6 { Trading on f6 gives up your bishop and the pin. } 8... Qxf6 9. Bxc6 dxc6 { Level: his queen and two bishops are free, and your centre is under fire. [%csl Rd4] }) (8. Be3 { Stepping back to e3 loosens your centre, and a pawn falls. } 8... exd4 9. cxd4 Nxe4 { He won a pawn, and your centre has cracked. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Bc1 { Going all the way home wastes two moves. } 8... exd4 9. e5 Nd5 10. cxd4 { Material is level again, but his knight sits on d5 and you have spent two moves on the bishop. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rd5] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: he takes on e4"]
[Result "1-0"]
