[FEN "r1bqkb1r/1ppp1ppp/p1n2n2/4p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 3 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/1ppp1ppp/p1n2n2/4p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1_b_kq_-_3_5"]

{ You have castled, and your pawn on e4 stands alone against his knight. }
5... Nxe4 { He takes your pawn on e4. Time to show why you let him. } 6. d4 { Yes: the d pawn strikes at e5 while his king is still in the middle. [%cal Gd4e5] } (6. Re1 { The rook hits the knight, but here his knight jumps to c5 and attacks your bishop. } 6... Nc5 7. Bxc6 dxc6 8. Nxe5 Be7 { Level: the pawn is back, but he has two bishops. The d pawn strike keeps your bishop alive. [%csl Bc8,Be7] }) (6. Qe2 { The queen hits the knight, and he answers with a hit on your bishop. } 6... Nc5 7. Bxc6 dxc6 8. d4 Ne6 { You are still a pawn down, facing two bishops and a knight on e6. Level, but you have the pawn to win back. [%csl Re6] }) (6. d3 { Kicking the knight with a pawn is too slow. } 6... Nc5 7. Bxc6 dxc6 8. Nxe5 Be7 { You won the pawn back, but he has the two bishops and more room. Slightly better for him. [%csl Bc8] }) 6... b5 { His b pawn kicks your bishop, so it steps back. Taking on d4 instead would walk into rook e1, pinning his knight to his king. } 7. Bb3 { Back to b3, where it looks along the diagonal at f7. [%cal Gb3f7] } 7... d5 { He props up his knight with the d pawn; taking on d4 would walk into rook e1 and the pin. You are still a pawn short. } 8. dxe5 { Material is level again: he took your e pawn, and you took his. Your pawn on e5 keeps his pieces off two good squares. [%csl Gd6,Gf6] } (8. Nxe5 { Taking with the knight is fine too, but it lets him trade. } 8... Nxe5 9. dxe5 c6 { Level: fewer pieces and a quieter game. The pawn capture keeps more tension. [%csl Be5] }) (8. Re1 { The rook to e1 is fine, and the pawn comes back a move later. } 8... Be6 9. dxe5 { Level: the same pawn back, and his bishop has come to e6. [%csl Be6] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: he kicks the bishop early"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/dLS7Obv0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/1ppp1ppp/p1n2n2/4p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 3 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/1ppp1ppp/p1n2n2/4p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQ1RK1_b_kq_-_3_5"]

{ You have castled, and your bishop on a4 still leans on his knight. }
5... b5 { His b pawn kicks your bishop at once, so back it goes. } 6. Bb3 { Back to b3, looking along the diagonal at f7. Your king pawn looks loose, but if his knight takes it, your rook comes to the e column. [%cal Gb3f7] } (6. Nxe5 { Grabbing e5 while your bishop is attacked costs a piece. } 6... bxa4 7. d4 Be7 { A pawn for your bishop: he is a piece up. [%csl Ra4] }) 6... Bc5 { His bishop comes out and eyes your king's side. On e7 it would have allowed your main line, and on b7 you would guard your king pawn with the rook first. And here? [%csl Be4] } 7. c3 { Good: the pawn prepares d4, which will stand in his bishop's way. Grabbing your king pawn now would not help him: that push hits back at once. [%cal Gc3d4] } (7. a4 { Just as good: the a pawn hits his b5 pawn. } 7... Rb8 8. c3 { Level: his rook now guards b5, and your plan with the c and d pawns goes on. [%cal Bb8b5] }) (7. d3 { Solid, but a pawn on d3 cannot block his bishop. } 7... d6 8. c3 { Level: a quiet game where his bishop on c5 stays strong. [%csl Bc5] }) (7. Nxe5 { Taking on e5 and forking with the d pawn works, but only just. } 7... Nxe5 8. d4 Bxd4 9. Qxd4 d6 { Level: material is even, and his knight on e5 stands well. [%csl Be5] }) (7. Re1 { A good move, and d4 can wait. } 7... O-O 8. c3 { Level: the same plan, a move later. [%csl Be1] }) (7. h3 { A useful move, but it does nothing about his bishop. } 7... O-O 8. c3 { Level: c3 and d4 still come, one move later than they could. [%csl Bh3] }) 7... d6 { He props up e5 with his d pawn. } 8. d4 { There it is: the pawn on d4 blocks his bishop's road to f2 and attacks it. Your king is castled, and his is still in the middle. [%cal Rc5f2,Gd4c5][%csl Re8] } (8. a4 { Hitting b5 is just as good. } 8... Bb7 9. d3 { Level: a slower plan, and his bishop on c5 still sees f2. [%csl Bc5] }) (8. Re1 { Fine, but d4 is ready right now. } 8... Bb6 9. d4 { Level: the same pawn to d4, one move later. [%csl Bb6] }) (8. h3 { A useful move, but d4 is the point. } 8... O-O 9. d4 Bb6 { Level: the same centre, a move later. [%csl Bb6] }) (8. d3 { Quiet, and it gives up the idea of standing in his bishop's way. } 8... O-O 9. Re1 { Level: a slow set-up, and his bishop on c5 keeps its road to f2. [%cal Rc5f2] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: he castles too soon"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/HGvOKY5v"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/1pppbppp/p1n2n2/4p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQR1K1 b kq - 5 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/1pppbppp/p1n2n2/4p3/B3P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQR1K1_b_kq_-_5_6"]

{ Your rook stands on e1, right behind your pawn on e4. }
6... O-O { He castles, and that is a mistake. Can you punish it? } 7. Bxc6 { Yes: take the knight that guards e5. [%cal Gb5c6,Gf3e5] } (7. c3 { A good move in the main line, but here it misses a free pawn. } 7... b5 8. Bb3 d6 { Level: he kicked your bishop and guarded e5, and the chance is gone. [%csl Re5] }) (7. Bb3 { Stepping back on your own lets him off the hook. } 7... d6 8. c3 { Level: e5 is guarded again, and the free pawn has gone. [%csl Rd6] }) (7. d3 { Solid, but it misses the moment. } 7... b5 8. Bb3 d6 { Level: his pawn on d6 guards e5 again. [%cal Rd6e5] }) 7... dxc6 { He takes back with the d pawn, and e5 has lost its only guard. With the b pawn it would be no different. } 8. Nxe5 { A clean pawn: e5 is yours, and your knight stands in the middle. [%csl Ge5] } (8. d3 { A solid move, but it leaves the free pawn alone. } 8... Nd7 { Level: the pawn on e5 got away, and he keeps the two bishops. [%csl Re5] }) (8. Nc3 { Developing looks natural, but it misses the free pawn. } 8... Nd7 9. d4 { Level: e5 is still standing, and he has the two bishops. [%csl Rc8] }) 8... Re8 { His rook comes to e8, lined up behind his bishop toward your knight. A bishop move was his other try, and a queen move to d4 could never win your king pawn while your rook guards it. [%cal Re8e5,Be1e4] } 9. Nf3 { Safe on f3: you are a clean pawn up, and your rook still guards your king pawn. He should have kicked your bishop with b5 before castling. [%csl Ge4] } (9. d3 { Just as good: the pawn guards e4 and opens your bishop. [%cal Bd3e4] } 9... Bb4 10. c3 Bd6 11. Nf3 { Still a clean pawn up: the knight had to step back to f3 anyway. [%csl Gf3] }) (9. d4 { Guarding the knight with a pawn looks solid, but his pieces get busy. } 9... c5 10. c3 cxd4 11. cxd4 { Still a pawn up, but his pieces are free and the edge is smaller. [%csl Rd4] }) (9. Nd3 { The knight on d3 blocks your d pawn. } 9... Qd4 10. Qf3 Bd6 { A pawn up still, but your pieces are tangled and the edge is small. [%csl Rd3] }) (9. Nc3 { Developing is natural, but his bishop pins and swaps. } 9... Bb4 10. Nd3 Bxc3 11. dxc3 Rxe4 12. Rxe4 Nxe4 { The pawn went back to him and a pair of rooks came off: material is level. [%csl Re4] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: dodge the Marshall"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/qLgUaalO"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/2ppbppp/p1n2n2/1p2p3/4P3/1B3N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQR1K1 b kq - 1 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/2ppbppp/p1n2n2/1p2p3/4P3/1B3N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQR1K1_b_kq_-_1_7"]

{ Your bishop has just stepped back to b3, as in the main line. }
7... O-O { He castles before d6, so his d pawn can jump two squares: the Marshall gambit, a pawn given to open lines at your king. Dodge it with h3. [%cal Yh2h3,Rd7d5] } 8. h3 { A quiet move: it keeps his bishop off g4, and your c pawn waits. His knight cannot chase your bishop yet: leaving its square would drop e5. [%csl Gg4] } (8. c3 { A perfectly good move, and the most played, but now he can play the Marshall gambit. } 8... d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6 { You won his e pawn, but his pieces are ready to pour at your king. That is the Marshall, a study of its own. [%cal Rd8h4] }) (8. a4 { Just as good: hitting his b5 pawn keeps him busy. } 8... b4 9. d3 d6 { Level and calm: his b pawn ran past, and no gambit came. [%csl Gd3] }) (8. d3 { Guarding e4 at once is also fine. [%cal Bd3e4] } 8... d6 9. a4 { Level: a quiet set-up with the pawn on d3 and no gambit. [%csl Gd3] }) (8. d4 { Striking in the middle now lets him trade on d4. } 8... Nxd4 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. e5 Ne8 11. Qxd4 { Level: the knights came off and your queen stands in the middle, but the big centre you wanted is gone. [%csl Bd4] }) (8. a3 { A hole on a2 for the bishop is a playable idea. } 8... d6 9. c3 { Level: with his d pawn one step up, c3 brings back a main line shape, plus your pawn on a3. [%csl Ba3] }) 8... d6 { He plays d6 after all, so his d pawn can no longer jump two squares. Now c3 is safe, and it brings back the main line. [%cal Yc2c3] } 9. c3 { The main line position again, reached without giving him his gambit. You dodged the Marshall gambit. [%csl Gc3,Gh3] } (9. a4 { Also fine, but your bishop still has no retreat on c2. } 9... Na5 10. Ba2 c5 { Level: your bishop hid on a2, and the main line plan is gone. [%csl Ba2] }) (9. d3 { Solid, but with d6 played his knight can chase your bishop. } 9... Na5 10. Nbd2 Nxb3 11. axb3 { Level, but his knight swapped itself for your bishop. [%csl Rb3] }) (9. a3 { Making a hole on a2 for the bishop is fine, but slow. } 9... Na5 10. Ba2 c5 { Level: a quieter version of the main line, with your bishop on a2. [%csl Ba2] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: his bishop comes to b7"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/wQTRLGsr"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/2ppbppp/p1n2n2/1p2p3/4P3/1B3N1P/PPPP1PP1/RNBQR1K1 b - - 0 8"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/2ppbppp/p1n2n2/1p2p3/4P3/1B3N1P/PPPP1PP1/RNBQR1K1_b_-_-_0_8"]

{ You played the little h pawn move and kept your c pawn at home. }
8... Bb7 { His bishop comes to b7, and his d pawn is still free to jump to d5. How do you keep the gambit out? } 9. d3 { Right: your d pawn guards e4. If he pushes d5 now, the pawns just trade, and nobody forces you to grab one. [%cal Gd3e4] } (9. c3 { Just as good by itself, but now his d5 push comes with play. } 9... d5 10. exd5 Nxd5 { Level, but the gambit you dodged is back: his pieces rush toward your king. [%csl Rd5] }) (9. Nc3 { The knight guards e4, but it blocks your c pawn for good. [%cal Bc3e4] } 9... d6 10. a3 { Level, but the c3 plans of the main line are gone. [%csl Rc3] }) (9. a4 { Hitting b5 is natural, but his knight hits your bishop first. } 9... Na5 10. Ba2 d5 { Level: his d5 push arrived anyway, and the game is sharp. [%csl Rd5] }) (9. a3 { Making room on a2 lets his d pawn jump at once. } 9... d5 10. exd5 Nd4 11. c4 Nxb3 12. Qxb3 { Level: you won a pawn, but his pieces spring to life, the sharp game you wanted to avoid. [%csl Rd5] }) 9... d6 { He props up e5 with his d pawn. Had he pushed d5, the pawns would trade and you would just develop. Your bishop has no retreat square yet. } 10. a3 { Right: a3 makes a home for your bishop, so it can stay on its diagonal toward f7. [%cal Gb3a2,Ga2f7] } (10. c3 { Just as good: the bishop gets c2 instead. } 10... Na5 11. Bc2 c5 { Level: a main line shape with your pawn on d3. We drill a3, so the bishop keeps its diagonal. [%csl Gc2] }) (10. a4 { Just as good: the pawn hits b5, and a2 opens for the bishop too. } 10... Na5 11. Ba2 c5 { Level: the same retreat to a2, with your a pawn further up. [%csl Ga2] }) (10. Nc3 { The knight is natural, but your bishop still has nowhere to hide. } 10... Na5 11. Ne2 Nxb3 12. axb3 { About level, but his knight swapped itself for your bishop, which had no square to hide on. [%csl Rb3] }) (10. Bd2 { Just as good: the bishop on d2 watches a5. [%cal Bd2a5] } 10... Qd7 11. a4 { Level: his knight cannot come to a5 while your bishop watches it, so your bishop on b3 is safe. [%cal Bd2a5] }) 10... Na5 { His knight goes after your bishop. Had he played queen d7 or knight b8, you would just bring out a knight. } 11. Ba2 { Safe: from a2 the bishop still stares at f7, and his knight sits on the edge of the board. [%cal Ga2f7][%csl Ra5] } (11. Nbd2 { Developing looks natural, but your bishop is still attacked. } 11... Nxb3 12. Nxb3 { He swapped his knight for your bishop, the piece that aimed at f7. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rb3] }) (11. c3 { The pawn gives the bishop c2, one move too late. } 11... Nxb3 12. Qxb3 { His knight took your bishop before it could use c2. Slightly better for him: your best piece is gone. [%csl Rb3] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: his knight chases your bishop"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/59Ubrsrk"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
