[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/2p1bppp/p1np1n2/1p2p3/4P3/1BP2N1P/PP1P1PP1/RNBQR1K1_b_-_-_0_9"]

{ The main line set-up is complete, with your pawns on c3 and h3. }
9... Na5 { His knight jumps to the edge and attacks your bishop. Had he played knight b8 or bishop b7, you would strike in the middle at once. } 10. Bc2 { Right: your c pawn cleared c2 for exactly this. Your bishop is safe, tucked behind your king pawn. [%cal Gb3c2] } (10. d4 { Striking now lets his knight swap itself for your bishop. } 10... Nxb3 11. axb3 Bb7 { Level, but your bishop is gone, the one piece that aimed at f7. [%csl Rb3] }) (10. d3 { Guarding e4 first gives him time to take the bishop. [%cal Bd3e4] } 10... Nxb3 11. axb3 c5 { Level, and the bishop from b3 is off the board. [%csl Rb3] }) (10. a4 { Hitting b5 does not save the bishop. } 10... Nxb3 11. Qxb3 Bb7 { He took your bishop, and your a pawn push achieved little. [%csl Ra4] }) 10... c5 { His c pawn comes to c5 and grabs room on the queen side. } 11. d4 { Three pawns in the middle, your bishop safe on c2, and his knight stuck on the edge of the board. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge4,Ra5] } (11. d3 { Solid, but it gives up the chance to take the middle in one step. } 11... Re8 12. Nbd2 Nc6 { Level: a slower game, and his knight got back from the edge. [%csl Rc6] }) (11. a4 { Hitting b5 is playable, but the middle matters more. } 11... Bd7 12. d3 { Level: slower, with his knight still on a5. [%csl Ba5] }) 1-0


[Event "Ruy Lopez: answer the pawn on d6"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/KWEynsxD/X7NLJ9NB"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/1Bb1p3/4P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 b kq - 2 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/1Bb1p3/4P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQ1RK1_b_kq_-_2_5"]

{ You have castled, and your bishop on b5 still looks at his knight on c6. }
5... d6 { His d pawn steps up to guard e5, and that is a mistake: it opens the line from your bishop to his king. What now? [%cal Bd6e5] } 6. d4 { Right: your pawn hits his bishop and his e pawn at once, and his knight cannot help, because it is pinned to his king. [%cal Gd4c5,Gd4e5,Gb5e8] } (6. Re1 { The rook guards e4, but it gives him a move to castle. [%cal Ge1e4] } 6... O-O { Level: he castled, so his knight is free again. [%csl Bg8] }) (6. h3 { A useful move, but it lets him castle out of the pin. } 6... O-O { Level: his king left the pin line while you spent a move on the h pawn. [%csl Bh3] }) (6. d3 { Solid, but a pawn on d3 hits nothing. } 6... O-O { Level: a slow set-up, and he castled in time. [%csl Bd3] }) (6. Bxc6+ { Taking the knight at once gives up the pin. } 6... bxc6 7. d4 Bb6 { Level: your bishop is gone, and his pawn on c6 now guards d5. [%cal Rc6d5] }) 6... Bb6 { His bishop steps back to b6. Most players take on d4 instead, and your c pawn takes back first. His knight is still pinned. What now? } 7. d5 { Right: the pawn hits the pinned knight, and it cannot move. [%cal Gd5c6,Gb5e8] } (7. dxe5 { Taking on e5 is natural, but it lets his knight take e4. } 7... Nxe4 8. Qd5 Nc5 { Slightly better for you: he won the pawn back, and your pin brought you nothing. [%csl Bc5] }) (7. Bg5 { Pinning his other knight is natural, but it gives him time to castle. } 7... O-O { Level: he castled, and his queen side knight is free again. [%csl Bg8] }) (7. Be3 { The bishop comes out, but the knight will not stay pinned for long. } 7... O-O { Level: one quiet move, and he castled out of the pin. [%csl Be3] }) (7. Nbd2 { Developing is fine, but the pin needs you now. } 7... O-O { Level: he castled while your knight came out. [%csl Bd2] }) (7. Re1 { The rook guards e4, but he castles before you push. [%cal Ge1e4] } 7... O-O { Level: the same position a move later, with his king out of the pin. [%csl Be1] }) (7. Bxc6+ { Taking the knight now throws the pin away. } 7... bxc6 8. dxe5 Nxe4 { Level: the trades left material even, and his knight stands well on e4. [%csl Be4] }) 7... a6 { His a pawn hits your bishop, to break the pin. Where does your bishop go? [%cal Ra6b5] } 8. Ba4 { Right: from a4 the bishop keeps the pin, and your pawn still hits the knight. Now watch. [%cal Ga4e8][%tail] } (8. dxc6 { Taking the knight at once lets his a pawn take your bishop. } 8... axb5 9. cxb7 Bxb7 { Better for him: the trades left material even, and his two bishops aim at your king. [%cal Rb7e4,Rb6f2] }) (8. Bxc6+ { Giving up the bishop throws away the pin. } 8... bxc6 9. dxc6 Nxe4 { Better for him: the pin is gone, and his knight took e4. [%csl Re4] }) (8. Bd3 { Stepping back to d3 lets the knight go. } 8... Ne7 { Level: your bishop left the pin, and his knight slipped away to e7. [%csl Be7] }) (8. Bc4 { From c4 the bishop no longer pins anything. } 8... Ne7 { Level: the pin is broken, and his knight is out of danger. [%csl Be7] }) (8. Be2 { The bishop runs home and gives up the pin. } 8... Ne7 { Level: his knight escaped, and your chance is gone. [%csl Be7] }) (8. Qa4 { The queen adds to the pin, but his a pawn takes your bishop. } 8... axb5 9. Qxa8 Ne7 { Slightly better for you: you won his rook for your bishop, but your queen is stuck in the corner. [%csl Ra8] }) 8... Nxe4 9. dxc6 { Your pawn takes the knight: a piece for a pawn. Had he castled before pushing d6, his king would have been off the pin line. [%csl Gc6] } 1-0
